 "honest": [
  "two cost levels: x0 switches the engine fee off entirely, so a gap that survives x0 is a price-path effect and not the cost of trading on that date; the x3 column is the same sweep with the fee switched back on",
  "the five days are fixed dates, not a fitted optimum: no date here is searched for and none may be read as a recommendation",
  "both calendars share one universe and one rank cache, so the only moving part is the set of decision sessions",
  "gap spread across days is a range statistic over five points, not a test",
  "descriptive census only: no gate, no verdict, no prereg, no trial count",
  "the weekly calendar is the ISO first-session grid (Monday-anchored); a month-end decision and a weekly decision almost never land on the same session, so 'overlap' here means proximity, not the same trade",
  "the weekly book rebalances ~4.3x more often than month-end, so a return gap against it mixes calendar with trading frequency; only `week_near_me` holds frequency fixed and prices the DATE",
  "`week_near_me` snaps each month-end to the nearest weekly session, so its decisions sit 1-3 sessions inside the boundary rather than on it -- the residual offset is the price of being on the weekly grid, not noise"
 ]
}
